WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.323 --> 00:00:01.968
- Hi my name is Claire.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.226
Do a little dance.

00:00:03.226 --> 00:00:04.059
Terrible!

00:00:06.019 --> 00:00:08.179
I hope what my YouTube videos have done

00:00:08.179 --> 00:00:09.683
is help people realize that they can be

00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:11.619
a normal human being.

00:00:11.619 --> 00:00:13.821
They can be whatever
the hell they wanna be,

00:00:13.821 --> 00:00:14.821
and be sick.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.213
I don't think that I've lived
any less of a beautiful life

00:00:19.213 --> 00:00:21.713
than your average healthy Joe.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.313
My name is Claire Wineland,

00:00:30.313 --> 00:00:32.450
and I have cystic fibrosis.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.564
Cystic fibrosis is a genetic disease,

00:00:34.564 --> 00:00:36.383
both terminal and progressive,

00:00:36.383 --> 00:00:38.345
which means that as you get older

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:40.088
cystic fibrosis gets worse.

00:00:40.088 --> 00:00:42.325
And it's also terminal, which
means that downward hill

00:00:42.325 --> 00:00:45.506
will end, in death. (chuckles)

00:00:45.506 --> 00:00:49.026
When I was born, my life
expectancy was around 10 years old.

00:00:49.026 --> 00:00:52.050
So then life expectancy was
pushed to 18, and then 19,

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.050
and now mid to late 20s.

00:00:55.112 --> 00:00:58.104
I realized a lot of people
with CF are really ashamed

00:00:58.104 --> 00:00:59.523
of having CF.

00:00:59.523 --> 00:01:01.650
It doesn't have to be like
this dark hidden secret

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.545
that you're dying.

00:01:03.545 --> 00:01:06.269
On my YouTube channel
I've covered stuff like

00:01:06.269 --> 00:01:08.050
what it's like to actually be in a coma,

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.528
how to talk to people who are sick,

00:01:09.528 --> 00:01:11.763
what not to say to people who are sick,

00:01:11.763 --> 00:01:12.909
stuff like that.

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:14.130
Sorry this was so depressing.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.230
Here's a little joke to make it better.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.071
What is, (clears throat)

00:01:18.071 --> 00:01:21.634
the biggest downside of
sudden unexpected death?

00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:24.152
Being unable to delete
your search history.

00:01:24.152 --> 00:01:26.210
Trying to explain how
cystic fibrosis feels

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.677
without sounding like
you're being overly dramatic

00:01:28.677 --> 00:01:30.608
and depressing is hard.

00:01:30.608 --> 00:01:34.653
It really feels like you
have sand in your lungs,

00:01:34.653 --> 00:01:37.912
and overall you just feel heavy and tired.

00:01:37.912 --> 00:01:41.474
My friends say I'm like a
grumpy old man. (laughs)

00:01:41.474 --> 00:01:43.010
And it's interesting looking at,

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.250
you know, the way that other
people live their lives,

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.413
which is, you know, they've never
really had to think about death.

00:01:48.413 --> 00:01:50.930
That's never been a
constant in their life.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.792
So I think fundamentally
at the end of the day,

00:01:52.792 --> 00:01:54.914
it doesn't matter really
whether you're scared of death

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.834
or whether you're scared
of paying your bills,

00:01:56.834 --> 00:01:59.537
we all have things in our life that are

00:01:59.537 --> 00:02:01.810
just completely foreign.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.307
That's the reason that death is so scary

00:02:03.307 --> 00:02:05.547
is 'cause it's so foreign.

00:02:05.547 --> 00:02:09.368
I think I want people to
take away from my life

00:02:09.368 --> 00:02:10.701
the notion that,

00:02:12.008 --> 00:02:15.229
they can live lives that they're proud of,

00:02:15.229 --> 00:02:19.146
whether or not they think
their life is normal.

